! Silicon, two atoms, one k-point, at a production cutoff.
!
! The same cell as si-1k.in with ecutwfc raised from 12 Ry to 40 Ry, which takes
! the basis from 180 plane waves to about a thousand. 12 Ry is what QE's test
! suite uses and is far too small to say anything about how either code scales:
! at that size both are dominated by fixed overheads. This case is where the
! choice of eigensolver starts to show, since a direct diagonalisation costs
! O(npw^3) and an iterative one does not.
!
! conv_thr is tightened well past what either code needs so that both run to the
! same fixed point: the comparison then reports a cost per iteration that means
! the same thing on both sides, and the two total energies agreeing to 1e-8 Ry
! doubles as a correctness check on every optimisation.
 &control
    calculation = 'scf'
 /
 &system
    ibrav=2, celldm(1) =10.20,
    nat=2, ntyp=1,
    ecutwfc=40.0
 /
 &electrons
    conv_thr = 1.0d-10
 /
ATOMIC_SPECIES
 Si  28.086  Si.pz-vbc.UPF
ATOMIC_POSITIONS (alat)
 Si 0.00 0.00 0.00
 Si 0.25 0.25 0.25
K_POINTS
  1
   0.250000  0.250000  0.250000   1.00
